so you want to make a map well lucky for  you far cry 5 includes a robust set of  tools that will allow you to craft your  very own playground of destruction it  can be overwhelming at first but once  you get the hang of it you can lay down  the skeleton for a map in minutes  however if you are new to this feature  there are some practices that in most  cases you should avoid number one don't  be flat for [ __ ] sake  yes flat maps can work yes it can be a  pain to carve out terrain and yes you  can still make a flat map interesting  however when was the last time you took  a walk outside nature isn't flat just  adding some elevation can make a world  of difference make rolling hills dig  valleys mould rivers and don't forget to  use the smooth tool to even out the  rough edges if you don't want to morph  the terrain then use one of the presets  you can select at the beginning number 2  don't make it too big it's tempting to  make a huge map trust me I've been there  many times but big maps present a whole  new set of challenge the bigger the map  is the longer it's going to take to fill  that map with interesting things if you  do have the time to make a big map you  also need to consider your budget these  bars in the corner here you can only fit  so much inside your map for example  here's an empty map that I'm going to  cover with trees as you can see that  exhausted my budget fairly quickly I  would recommend starting small with your  map and growing outwards number 3 don't  forget boundaries this is typically one  of the last things I do when making a  map once you've figured out the size of  your map switch to the map limits tool  this will give players a death countdown  if they get a little too adventurous  number 4 don't be bland one thing that  separates the top rated maps from the  rest are that they have textures it's  super easy to add textures and it can  make a world of difference you can even  change the default texture to whatever  you want within seconds just click on  the grid texture hit change and pick  whatever you want  number five don't be an ass  difficulty is good no one wants a  cakewalk up a map unless you're trying  to grind for XP but spawning me and a  tiny pit filled with yetis or heavy  enemies is not fun try to ramp up  difficulty as the player progresses  through the map also don't cram your map  with 80-plus enemies and ask the player  to kill everyone there are exceptions to  this such as the map called Semper Fi  but for the most part be reasonable  number six don't make dumb enemies while  it's not the most intuitive set of tools  you do have some flexibility with enemy  AI you can give them things to do areas  to defend and places to go this is  something that can wait until the end  but if you are trying to make a map that  tells the story and feels alive it's  definitely an important step number  seven don't forget about environment the  environment tools are how you set the  mood of your map you can adjust time of  day wind force fog the backdrop and post  effects seriously this is what brings  your map to life in my opinion you can  turn a simple collection of trees into a  dark eerie forest with just a couple  sliders now as you know rules are meant  to be broken and I'm sure with some  digging you can find exceptions to these  rules however as you create your own  Maps these are some solid guidelines to  follow especially if you're just  starting out if you have any tips leave  them in the comments below and if you  have any questions feel free to hit me  up on Twitter thank you for watching  [Music]
 

